<p>To check the felt effect in numbers, <strong>15 development tasks</strong> by difficulty (basic · intermediate · advanced) were compared <strong>without a harness vs with one</strong>, and the final code was evaluated by an <strong>LLM judge</strong>.</p>
<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Result</span>
    On average <strong>about a 60% performance improvement</strong>, and a <strong>100% win rate</strong> when a harness was composed. Especially the <strong>harder</strong> the task &mdash; using only a single tool made the score drop a lot, but the harness had the effect of <strong>keeping the quality</strong>.
</div>
<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">About the numbers</span>
    The numbers above are not a public benchmark but the <strong>speaker's own measurements</strong>. For reference, Anthropic has also announced that a multi-agent configuration showed a <strong>90.2% performance improvement</strong> over a single agent in its internal evaluation (multi-agent research system).
</div>
